 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Catastrophic Forgetting:** Training a single NeRF sequentially on subsets of rays causes loss of information on previously seen domains. This is mitigated by using *non-overlapping* NeRFs for each tile, ensuring learned information is persistent and not overwritten [S1].
-*   **Tile Edges:** Rendering errors can occur along tile boundaries if rays are not handled correctly. The segmented sampler and 2x2 sliding window are specifically designed to prevent 3D reconstruction errors at these edges [S1].
+*   **Catastrophic Forgetting:** Training a single NeRF sequentially on subsets of rays $R_i$ causes loss of information on previously seen domains $R_j$ ($j < i$) [S1]. The framework avoids this by using non-overlapping NeRFs and the sliding window mechanism [S1].
+*   **Memory Limits:** A typical scene (10km x 10km, 20 images) requires ~200GB for images, ~600GB for the NeRF, and 1.4TB for ray vectors (float32), which exceeds single GPU memory [S1]. The on-the-fly ray computation and out-of-core loading address this [S1].
 
